[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "2608180536"
  :adr/title "@etzhayyim/sdk is a type-only dependency declared as a runtime one, and it is why app-live will not install"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-18"
  :adr/repo "cloud-itonami/app-live"
  :adr/measured-on "darwin-arm64, Node v26.3.0, npm 11.16.0, pnpm 10.26.2"
  :adr/base-commit "9c5330185d8cc73ee2dec7f838e324f81cae5c19"
  :adr/context
  "kotoba/package.json declares @etzhayyim/sdk in :dependencies (git+https, pinned
   12314a0cc5ac2feb49dd9789d5c002398acb6988). No install in that directory succeeds.

   Measured, in this order:

   1. `npm install` -> code 1, 'git dep preparation failed', EALLOWSCRIPTS:
      '--allow-scripts is not allowed in project-scoped installs'. npm >= 11.16
      refuses the nested install it uses to prepare a git dependency.
   2. `npm install --ignore-scripts` -> identical failure. The flag does not
      reach the preparation subprocess, which spawns its own install with
      --force --allow-scripts.
   3. `npm install vitest typescript` (unrelated packages) -> identical failure.
      npm prepares declared git deps regardless of what is being added, so the
      directory is unusable for any install at all.
   4. `pnpm install --ignore-scripts` -> ERR_PNPM_MISSING_PACKAGE_NAME on a
      transitive dep, git+https://github.com/kotoba-lang/ipfs.git#main.
   5. Both git remotes resolve (git ls-remote succeeds), so this is not an
      access or availability problem.

   Why the sdk needs preparation at all: its package.json sets
   main ./dist/index.js with 'prepare': 'tsc', and its dependency tree includes
   viem, @atproto/*, and six further kotoba-lang git dependencies."

  :adr/decision
  "Record the finding here and document the working path in
   docs/operator-quickstart.md. Do NOT work around it by editing
   kotoba/package.json in this repository.

   The decisive measurement is that the sdk is not needed at runtime:

   - kotoba/src/registry.ts imports it as `import type { Etzhayyim }`, which
     TypeScript erases before emit.
   - @etzhayyim/sdk-mock, which the tests actually construct, has zero import
     statements — it declares its own interfaces and MockEtzhayyim class.

   Verified by running the suite with vitest 4.1.10 grafted in from outside the
   project and the pinned sdk-mock commit
   (c857ff9be5310bf433bfe1e8d3c0f677e213d667) cloned into node_modules, and no
   @etzhayyim/sdk present anywhere: Test Files 1 passed, Tests 3 passed (3).

   So the package is uninstallable because of a dependency none of its runtime
   code loads."

  :adr/why-not-fixed-here
  "A local edit cannot fix it. @etzhayyim/sdk-mock's own package.json declares
   @etzhayyim/sdk in its :dependencies, so installing the mock pulls the sdk in
   regardless of what app-live declares. Moving the entry to :devDependencies in
   this repo changes nothing — it is still installed, and still prepared.

   The fix belongs upstream, and there is a choice to be made there:
     (a) publish @etzhayyim/sdk with prebuilt dist/ and type declarations so no
         `prepare` step is required of consumers, or
     (b) drop the sdk from @etzhayyim/sdk-mock's :dependencies, since the mock
         does not import it, and let consumers depend on the sdk only for types.

   Either removes the blocker for every kotoba reference implementation that
   uses this mock, not just this one. app-live is not the right place to decide
   which."

  :adr/consequences
  "Known and accepted, until upstream moves:

   - Tests run, via the grafted-toolchain path in docs/operator-quickstart.md.
     3/3 pass.
   - `tsc --noEmit` does not pass. Without the sdk it reports TS2307 on
     src/registry.ts:7 plus four TS7006 implicit-any errors at
     src/registry.ts:112, 124, 161, 168. There is one root cause; the four are
     downstream of the unresolved Etzhayyim type.
   - Those implicit-any errors must not be silenced with explicit `any`
     annotations. Doing so would hide the missing dependency while leaving the
     package equally uninstallable.
   - CI that runs `npm ci` or `npm install` against this package will fail on
     npm >= 11.16, and the error text names scripts rather than the real cause,
     which is why it is written out in full above."

  :adr/scope
  "This ADR records a dependency-declaration finding. It does not change the
   substrate posture, the public/regulated split, or the migration state
   described in MIGRATION-TODO.md and RESUME.edn."}]
